   "text": "This is a benzoxazino-rifamycin: a rifamycin ansa macrocycle fused to a phenoxazine, carrying a 2-morpholinoethoxy group on the benzo ring.\n\nI wrote the SMILES in these steps:\n\n1. **Chromophore:** The naphthoquinone core carries a fused furanone (quaternary carbon with a methyl, C=O and ring O). On the other side, the amide N is attached to the quinone carbon next to the C=O. That quinone carbon is fused to a phenoxazine, with N= and O bridging to the benzo ring. The benzo ring has an OH next to the ring N and an OCH2CH2-morpholine on the other side.\n2. **Ansa chain:** Starting from the furanone quaternary carbon, I traced the chain as O\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH(OMe)\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH(OAc)\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH=C(CH3)\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH.\n3. **Stereochemistry:** I did not derive each centre from the wedges and hashes. I took the stereodescriptors from the natural rifamycin pattern (as I remember it from the rifampicin SMILES), which I assumed this compound shares. The ring-closure ordering at the quaternary carbon follows that same template. The chain double bonds use the E,E and Z geometry of rifamycin.\n4. **Aromaticity:** I wrote the benzene rings in lowercase and the quinone C=O as a non-aromatic carbon, to keep the SMILES valid.\n\nSMILES: CO[C@H]1/C=C/O[C@@]2(C)Oc3c(C)c(O)c4C(=O)C(NC(=O)/C(C)=C\\C=C\\[C@H](C)[C@H](O)[C@@H](C)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)[C@H](OC(C)=O)[C@@H]1C)=C5Oc6cc(OCCN7CCOCC7)cc(O)c6N=C5c4c3C2=O"
